From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CogWasm <cogwasm@example.invalid>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 17:20:00 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: keep incremental artifacts in the serving guest

A serving rustc rebuilds and re-reads its incremental dep graph, query
cache and work-product index from disk on every edit. `-Zcogwasm-
incremental-memory` keeps those three artifacts in a process-local map
instead: a memory-backed FileEncoder publishes its finished bytes under a
stable key (the incremental crate directory plus the file name), and
`open_incremental_file` takes them before falling back to the file path.

The dep graph is renamed from its staging path before the encoder finishes,
so a rename of bytes that do not exist yet is recorded and applied when the
encoder publishes. Work-product object files stay on disk; only their index
is in memory.

The flag is off by default, untracked, and only has an effect on wasm32. A
one-shot process still compiles correctly: it publishes state and then exits
without a reader, which is the same fallback as a cold incremental start.

This is a local CogWasm experiment, not an upstream contribution; the code,
comments and message are LLM-generated under the Rust project's personal-use
exemption, and a human must review and own it before it goes anywhere else.

